From 4db30c27923ea79730e33d367305e9b403c5b70a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 21:55:12 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 07/94] cpufreq: qcom-hw: do not device-link a CPU to its CPRh
 domain

Attaching the CPRh power domain hangs MSM8998 before the driver has
touched a single register: no fuse read, no CPR write, no OSM write.
The whole hardware sequence is innocent, and the difference against the
downstream tree this was ported from is that the newer
dev_pm_domain_attach_list() adds a device link, while the OPP helper it
replaces did not - devm_pm_opp_attach_genpd() attached the domain,
stored the virtual device and stopped.

The link buys nothing here.  Its consumer is a CPU device, which never
probes and never runtime suspends, so there is no probe order and no
suspend order for the link to enforce, and its supplier is a domain
already marked always-on, which DL_FLAG_RPM_ACTIVE then asks to runtime
resume from a device that has no runtime PM of its own.

Ask for no link at all, which is what the old helper did.

Note for whoever assembles the series: once CPU frequency scaling works,
commit 92d769a1dc1e ("arm64: dts: qcom: msm8998-google-wahoo: describe
the measured CPU capacity") has to be reverted in the same series.  It
describes a gold cluster stuck near its lowest frequency, and stops
being true the moment this driver can raise it.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c b/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c
index 7912dfc40..951f271ac 100644
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c
@@ -545,11 +545,17 @@ static int qcom_cpufreq_gen_params(struct device *cpu_dev,
 	 * It must run while that table is still empty, which is why the CPRh
 	 * corner setup is deferred to qcom_cpr3_cprh_init() below instead of
 	 * happening in the domain's attach_dev callback.
+	 *
+	 * PD_FLAG_NO_DEV_LINK because the consumer here is a CPU device, which
+	 * never probes and never runtime suspends, so a device link to the
+	 * domain orders nothing.  The default link would runtime resume a
+	 * domain that is already always-on, from a device that has no runtime
+	 * PM of its own.
 	 */
 	struct dev_pm_domain_attach_data attach_data = {
 		.pd_names = cprh_genpd_names,
 		.num_pd_names = 1,
-		.pd_flags = PD_FLAG_DEV_LINK_ON | PD_FLAG_REQUIRED_OPP,
+		.pd_flags = PD_FLAG_NO_DEV_LINK | PD_FLAG_REQUIRED_OPP,
 	};
 	struct platform_device *pdev = cpufreq_get_driver_data();
 	const struct qcom_cpufreq_soc_data *soc_data = qcom_cpufreq.soc_data;
